WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.190
Every Monday, half my class arrives late.

2
00:00:03.820 --> 00:00:05.240
The head teacher asked me why.

3
00:00:05.910 --> 00:00:08.610
I had a guess: maybe pupils are

4
00:00:08.610 --> 00:00:09.850
tired after the weekend.

5
00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:13.260
But a guess is not an answer.

6
00:00:13.910 --> 00:00:15.690
So I asked the data.

7
00:00:17.010 --> 00:00:19.190
Today: what data science is,

8
00:00:19.570 --> 00:00:20.850
and how you can use it.

9
00:00:22.040 --> 00:00:24.400
Data science means using data

10
00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:25.460
to answer a question.

11
00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:28.800
Data engineers collect and store the data.

12
00:00:29.430 --> 00:00:32.090
Data scientists ask it questions, find

13
00:00:32.090 --> 00:00:34.190
patterns, and explain what they mean.

14
00:00:35.370 --> 00:00:37.910
Data science is a loop with four steps.

15
00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:40.160
Ask a clear question.

16
00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:42.380
Collect the data you need.

17
00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:45.040
Explore it with numbers and charts.

18
00:00:45.860 --> 00:00:47.580
Then explain what you found,

19
00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:49.240
and ask the next question.

20
00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:53.220
I took my register and counted late

21
00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:56.240
arrivals for four weeks, day by day.

22
00:00:56.710 --> 00:00:59.230
Monday had twelve, the other

23
00:00:59.230 --> 00:01:00.690
days only about three.

24
00:01:01.330 --> 00:01:03.710
The pattern was clear, and it was not

25
00:01:03.710 --> 00:01:06.470
tiredness, because Friday was fine too.

26
00:01:07.810 --> 00:01:10.990
So I asked the next question: how

27
00:01:10.990 --> 00:01:12.630
do the late pupils travel?

28
00:01:13.160 --> 00:01:14.500
Most came on the same bus.

29
00:01:15.250 --> 00:01:17.790
On Mondays, it leaves the market late.

30
00:01:18.550 --> 00:01:20.970
The data led us to the real cause.

31
00:01:22.420 --> 00:01:25.180
A good data question is clear, you

32
00:01:25.180 --> 00:01:27.320
can measure it, and it is about

33
00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:28.700
something you can act on.

34
00:01:29.290 --> 00:01:31.710
Are pupils happy is hard to measure.

35
00:01:32.410 --> 00:01:34.190
How many pupils arrive late,

36
00:01:34.530 --> 00:01:36.430
on which days, is easy.

37
00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:38.080
Quick check!

38
00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:40.400
Which is the better data question for your

39
00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:43.580
class: do we like reading, or how many

40
00:01:43.580 --> 00:01:45.200
books did each of us borrow this term?

41
00:01:46.330 --> 00:01:47.690
Books borrowed this term.

42
00:01:48.350 --> 00:01:49.730
You can count it from the library

43
00:01:49.730 --> 00:01:51.590
cards, and compare classes.

44
00:01:52.330 --> 00:01:55.350
Liking reading matters too, but you would

45
00:01:55.350 --> 00:01:57.430
need a careful survey to measure it.

46
00:01:58.510 --> 00:02:00.690
In the app, open Tidy tables.

47
00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:04.060
A messy sheet gives the wrong answers.

48
00:02:04.830 --> 00:02:07.310
Fix it step by step, and watch

49
00:02:07.310 --> 00:02:08.670
the answers become right.

50
00:02:09.920 --> 00:02:10.460
Your turn.

51
00:02:11.070 --> 00:02:12.470
We explained our finding.

52
00:02:13.230 --> 00:02:15.010
What comes next in the loop?

53
00:02:16.230 --> 00:02:17.010
A new question.

54
00:02:17.850 --> 00:02:19.490
Remember: data science

55
00:02:19.490 --> 00:02:21.270
answers questions with data.

56
00:02:21.940 --> 00:02:24.540
Ask, collect, explore,

57
00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:27.480
explain, and go round again.

58
00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:31.340
Try it yourself: write three questions

59
00:02:31.340 --> 00:02:34.060
about your school that data could answer.

60
00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:36.860
Choose one, count the answer this week,

61
00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:38.900
and say where your number came from.
